PREPRINT RULES
From the fact archive to cv.md (output/cv.md), and the one-page cuts
Owner: Alvaro Rioboo de Larriva
Created: 2026-11-10 as cv_preprogram_lint.rules; renamed to preprint.rules
and split into two flows on 2026-11-10.

These rules describe how the agent builds and checks cv.md (output/cv.md,
rule 0.3) from the fact archive, following the way career-ops works.
Parts 1 to 3 stop at cv.md. Part 4 holds the house rules for generating
CVs from it (the one-page cuts); everything else about generating a CV
(the one-page general CV, tailored CVs and any other version) follows
the project's rules (modes/pdf.md and its checks) and the agent's own
judgment, with the house settings in modes/_custom.md. Nothing here
sends or submits anything.

Layout:
  PART 1  Common rules, whatever the archive format
  PART 2  Flow A: cv.md from output/all_info.md (Markdown archive)
  PART 3  Flow B: cv.md from output/all_info.json (JSON archive)
  PART 4  After cv.md: generating CVs

Part 1 names archive concepts in [brackets]; Parts 2 and 3 say where each
one lives in that format.


######################################################################
PART 1. COMMON RULES
######################################################################

======================================================================
STAGE 0. SOURCES AND WHAT EACH ONE IS FOR
======================================================================

0.1  The [archive] holds every confirmed fact, each with its [sources]. It
     is never rendered directly.
0.2  Exactly one archive is active at a time:
     - Today: output/all_info.json, so Flow B (Part 3) applies.
     - Flow A (Part 2) applies only when output/all_info.md is the archive.
     - If both files exist, the agent asks Alvaro which one rules and never
       merges them silently. A change of format is a full conversion,
       after which the old file is removed.
0.3  cv.md is the curated source for every CV. In these rules "cv.md" is
     output/cv.md: the file these rules build from the archive, and the
     one every CV is generated from. Whatever it says is what every CV can
     claim.
0.3b The career-ops scripts and modes read the CV only from the root
     cv.md (pdf, latex, the fact gate, cv-title-check, verify-cv-structure,
     jd-skill-gap, doctor), never from output/ or the archive. So the root
     cv.md is an exact copy of output/cv.md, never edited on its own:
     - Every change is made in output/cv.md, then copied over the root
       cv.md in the same step (cp output/cv.md cv.md).
     - A tool that writes the root cv.md (add, expand) is not used for it;
       if the root copy ever differs, the agent stops and asks which
       change is right, makes it in output/cv.md and copies it again.
     - The Stage 4 check (B.4) fails while the two differ.
0.4  modes/_profile.md supplies the framing: the cross-cutting advantage
     ("Physicist with computational astrophysics training who applies data
     science and machine learning to real-world problems") and the
     emphasis per archetype.
0.5  modes/_custom.md supplies the house rules (English only, email first,
     confirmation before anything moves into cv.md).
0.6  config/profile.yml supplies identity and contact. Its page budget
     (cv.max_pages: 1) applies when a CV is generated, not to cv.md.
0.7  Out of scope for cv.md content (AGENTS.md, Source-of-Truth Boundary):
     agent memory, other projects, anything not written in the files above,
     and the LinkedIn profile itself (LinkedIn is a source for the archive,
     not for cv.md; the archive keeps the published profile for comparison
     only). A fact Alvaro states in chat goes into the archive first, as a
     [stated source] with the date, and only then into cv.md.


======================================================================
STAGE 1. SELECT: archive -> cv.md
======================================================================

1.1  Facts move from the archive into cv.md only after Alvaro confirms each
     move. cv.md is not limited to one page: fitting a page is done when
     each CV is generated (Part 4).
1.2  The agent proposes the move as a list: the fact, where it sits in the
     archive with its [sources], and where it would go in cv.md. Nothing is
     written before a yes.
1.3  NEVER moved into cv.md:
     - Sensitive information: phone numbers, secondary emails (the account
       email alvaroinator7@gmail.com, the UCM email), street addresses and
       any other private contact or personal data. The only contact details
       in cv.md are the professional email, LinkedIn, GitHub, the website
       and the city.
     - Anything in a [private section]: job search conditions
       (availability, work authorization, compensation, years of
       experience, preferences), gaps, search state and pending items.
       They serve evaluations, forms and interviews, never cv.md.
     - Anything [excluded] (today: the secondary emails and the Big Data &
       Business Analytics MSc, both at Alvaro's request).
     - Anything [pending confirmation].
1.4  Moved only with a concrete example ready: items that [need an
     example] (today: MATLAB, Hadoop).
1.5  Where sources disagree, cv.md uses the [decision] for that item. No
     other value.
1.6  No metric, scale figure or result is added unless the archive states
     it. It holds none today (see [gaps]: metrics), so cv.md carries none.
1.7  "Reformulate, never invent" (AGENTS.md): wording may be shortened,
     merged or reordered, but every claim must trace to the archive.
1.8  No claim of authorship of a tool, library or repo unless an [authored
     project] says so (today only covid19_dashboard).


======================================================================
STAGE 2. STRUCTURE (what the career-ops scripts need)
======================================================================

The checking scripts find content by heading (AGENTS.md, onboarding
Step 1; examples/cv-example.md). cv.md keeps this shape:

2.1  "# Full name", then the headline line, then the contact line.
2.2  Sections, in this order: ## Summary, ## Experience, ## Projects,
     ## Education, ## Certifications, ## Awards & Honors, ## Skills,
     ## Keywords.
2.3  The Experience section is headed exactly "## Experience".
2.4  Each job is "### Company — Location", then a bold "**Title**" line,
     then a dates line, then an optional "Department: {department}" line,
     then the bullets. The Department line is the job's [department] and is
     left out when the job has none. In the CV payload it becomes
     experience[].context. The em dash in the job header is required by
     the parser and is the only em dash allowed in cv.md.
2.5  Jobs are in reverse chronological order (generate-pdf.mjs refuses a
     CV whose roles are not newest-first).
2.6  Skills sit under a heading containing "Skills", one bold category per
     line: "- **Category:** item, item, item".
2.7  Each project is one line: "- **[name](url)**, Mon YYYY – Mon YYYY.
     {Description} with {stack}." The dates are the [project period],
     in a date field like every other dated entry, never inside the
     description. In the payload they go in projects[].badge, which both
     templates render as the entry's date column (output/build_payload.mjs).


======================================================================
STAGE 3. CONTENT
======================================================================

3.1  English only, never mixed. Institutions use the [standardized English
     name]. In Education it carries the [acronym] in parentheses
     ("University of La Laguna (ULL)"); in Experience job headers it does
     not (rule 3.9). Only proper names stay as they are: places (Córdoba,
     San Cristóbal de La Laguna), companies (Deuser Tech Group S.L.),
     schools, and Spanish qualifications with no exact English equivalent.
3.2  Each cv.md job comes from one [experience entry], in the same order:
     company = [employer], title = [role] exactly, Department line =
     [department], location = [place] ("Remote" for remote work), dates =
     [period]. cv-title-check later holds every tailored CV to these
     titles. Stage 4 checks this mapping.
3.3  Dates use "Mon YYYY – Mon YYYY".
3.4  The contact line starts with the email.
3.5  The Summary is one short, general paragraph that joins the physicist
     framing from modes/_profile.md with the AI context ("Physicist and AI
     practitioner ..."). No project-specific detail (models, projects,
     employers): that belongs in Experience. It holds no keyword list.
     Default summary (cv.md always holds this text):
       "Physicist and AI practitioner specialized in computational
       astrophysics, applying data analysis and machine learning across
       scientific and industrial projects. Detail-oriented, organized and
       driven team player, committed to delivering high-quality work."
     For a specific posting, the tailored CV starts from the default and
     changes it only slightly: name the target role, or swap in terms the
     posting uses that cv.md already backs (for example "predictive
     modelling" for a Data Scientist role, "deep learning" for a Deep
     Learning Engineer role). Same length, same structure, no new claims.
     The change lives in that CV's payload, never in cv.md.
3.6  The ## Keywords section is last: one comma-separated line of ATS
     keywords (disciplines and methods, not tools). Each keyword must be
     backed by an Experience entry, Projects, Education or Certifications
     in cv.md.
     In the payload this list is competencies[], with
     "sections": {"competencies": "Keywords"}. Both templates render it at
     the end in a soft grey.
3.7  Skills lists hold concrete tools, libraries and languages, never the
     disciplines already in the Keywords section. The Data & ML line does
     not repeat generic terms such as Machine Learning or Data Analysis.
     Category order: Programming, Data & ML, Databases & Cloud, Languages.
     Item order inside each line follows rule 3.8.
3.8  Ordering of Skills and Keywords by target role. Items are only
     reordered, never added or removed. The exceptions are the one-page
     cuts in Part 4 (Keywords and Skills), made in the order Part 4 sets:
     - With a posting: what the posting asks for comes first, in the order
       of its requirements (jd-skill-gap.mjs `existing` items first), then
       the rest in the default order. Category order may follow the
       posting too, Languages always last.
     - Without a posting, the closest archetype in modes/_profile.md
       decides; with no archetype stated, the default is Data Scientist.
     - cv.md itself always holds the default order: Data Scientist
       (statistics, predictive modelling, EDA, model evaluation first; deep
       learning and computer vision after; Python, R and SQL first;
       scikit-learn, pandas and NumPy before PyTorch and TensorFlow).
     - A tailored CV reorders in its payload, never in cv.md.
3.9  ATS parsing (ats-payload.mjs lints). In Experience:
     - The company in "### Company — Location" is only the employer's name:
       no acronym or other parenthetical, no list of platforms.
     - The **Title** line is only the job title: no department, group or
       employer name in it.
     - The department goes only in the Department line (rule 2.4), never
       in the title or the bullets.
     - Other details moved out of the header (such as the freelance
       platforms) go into a bullet.
     - Freelance work is listed under "Self-employed".
3.10 At most three bullets per job; each bullet is one fact, not a list of
     unrelated ones.
3.11 No item from rule 1.3.


======================================================================
STAGE 4. CHECK cv.md
======================================================================

4.1  The agent reads cv.md against every rule in Stages 1 to 3.
4.2  The archive check of the active flow passes (A.4 or B.4), including
     the root-copy check (rule 0.3b).
4.3  node doctor.mjs --json
     Its warnings must not say the Experience section was not found.
4.4  If any rule fails, the agent stops, lists the failures and asks before
     fixing cv.md.
4.5  When cv.md or the archive changes, the agent adds a dated entry to
     LOG_ARIOBOO.md.


######################################################################
PART 2. FLOW A: cv.md FROM output/all_info.md
######################################################################

Applies only when output/all_info.md is the active archive (rule 0.2).
Not active today.

A.1  Format. A Markdown document with these sections: Identity and
     contact, Positioning, Experience, Education, Certifications and
     courses, Skills, Languages, Chess, Memberships, Projects,
     Recommendations, LinkedIn profile as published, Job search context,
     Gaps and missing information, Search state, Pending confirmation,
     Source differences.
A.2  Where each concept lives:
     [sources]              Inline tags `PDF`, `LinkedIn`, `LinkedIn PDF`,
                            `Stated` + date, after the fact they back.
     [stated source]        `Stated` YYYY-MM-DD.
     [experience entry]     One row of the table at the top of
                            ## Experience: Employer | Role | Department |
                            Place | Dates | Type. The bullets are under
                            "### {Employer}".
     [employer], [acronym]  Employer column: "Name (ACRONYM)"; the name
                            without the parentheses is the employer.
     [department]           Department column; "-" means none.
     [role], [place],       Role, Place and Dates columns, dates as
     [period]               "Mon YYYY – Mon YYYY".
     [decision]             Decision column of the ## Source differences
                            table.
     [pending confirmation] ## Pending confirmation list.
     [excluded]             Items marked "Kept out of the CV at Alvaro's
                            request", plus the other emails in Identity.
     [need an example]      The "Only on LinkedIn, with no project or job
                            behind them" line under ## Skills.
     [authored project]     ## Projects.
     [project period]       Dates of the entry under ## Projects, as
                            "Mon YYYY – Mon YYYY".
     [gaps]                 ## Gaps and missing information.
     [private section]      ## Job search context, ## Gaps and missing
                            information, ## Search state, ## Pending
                            confirmation.
A.3  Writing. New facts go into the right section with their tag; a
     disagreement between sources becomes a row in ## Source differences.
     Missing values are written "-".
A.4  Check. There is no validator for this format. The agent compares each
     cv.md job with its Experience table row by hand (employer, role,
     Department line, dates) and lists any mismatch, then checks that the
     root cv.md is an exact copy (cmp output/cv.md cv.md, rule 0.3b).


######################################################################
PART 3. FLOW B: cv.md FROM output/all_info.json
######################################################################

Active today (rule 0.2).

B.1  Format. output/all_info.json, with its structure fixed by
     output/all_info.schema.json (JSON Schema draft 2020-12,
     schema_version 1.2.0). Conventions:
     - Keys and enum values in snake_case (full_time, self_employed).
     - Dates are "YYYY", "YYYY-MM" or "YYYY-MM-DD"; periods are
       {"start", "end"}.
     - A missing value is null, never "-", "—", "N/A" or "".
     - Every fact is {"text", "sources"}; a source is {"source": pdf |
       linkedin | linkedin_pdf | stated, "date"?, "note"?}.
     - cv_policy on an item: allowed | excluded | needs_example.
     - Every list entry that others refer to has a unique snake_case id.
     - Private sections are listed in meta.private_sections.
B.2  Where each concept lives:
     [sources]              "sources" array on each fact or entry.
     [stated source]        {"source": "stated", "date": "YYYY-MM-DD"}.
     [experience entry]     experience[] (newest first); bullets in
                            experience[].bullets.
     [employer], [acronym]  experience[].employer.name / .short_name
                            (education: institution.name / .short_name).
     [department]           experience[].department; null means none.
     [role]                 experience[].role.
     [place]                experience[].place {type, city, region,
                            country}; type "remote" -> "Remote".
     [period]               experience[].period {start, end}, "YYYY-MM",
                            written in cv.md as "Mon YYYY".
     [project period]       projects[].period {start, end}, "YYYY-MM".
     [decision]             source_differences[].decision. The main
                            sections already hold the decided values.
     [pending confirmation] pending_confirmation[].
     [excluded]             cv_policy: "excluded".
     [need an example]      cv_policy: "needs_example".
     [authored project]     projects[] with authored_by_candidate: true.
     [gaps]                 gaps[].
     [private section]      Every section listed in meta.private_sections
                            (today job_search, gaps, search_snapshot,
                            pending_confirmation).
B.3  Writing. Every change follows the schema: no unknown keys, no
     placeholders, sources on every fact. A disagreement between sources
     becomes a source_differences[] entry. A new kind of data needs a
     schema change first (bump schema_version).
B.4  Check.
     node output/validate_all_info.mjs --cv output/cv.md --mirror cv.md
     Run after every change to all_info.json or cv.md. It checks the JSON
     against the schema (types, enums, date formats, unknown keys, ids,
     cross-references, placeholders), that each cv.md job matches its
     experience entry (employer, role, Department line, dates), that each
     cv.md project carries its [project period], and that
     the root cv.md is an exact copy of output/cv.md (rule 0.3b). It must
     exit 0.


######################################################################
PART 4. AFTER cv.md: GENERATING CVs
######################################################################

- Every CV is generated from cv.md (output/cv.md, rule 0.3), following
  the project's rules (modes/pdf.md and its checks) and the agent's own
  judgment. The payload is built from output/cv.md, with every keyword,
  by node output/build_payload.mjs output/cv.md <payload.json>; the cuts
  and the ordering below are applied to that payload. The checks that
  read the root cv.md see the same text, because it is an exact copy
  (rule 0.3b). The house
  settings (file names, templates, photo, contact order) are in
  modes/_custom.md.
- Each generated CV selects and orders what cv.md says. The only
  rewording allowed is the tailored summary (rule 3.5) and the ask-first
  shortening below. Skills and Keywords are ordered for the target
  posting by rule 3.8 (default: Data Scientist). It never adds a claim
  cv.md does not hold, and never edits cv.md to make a page fit.
- A one-page CV is made to fit only by the cuts below, in their order:
  first the Keywords cut, then the ask-first list.
- For the one-page CV, if a render runs past one page, the agent first
  shortens the Keywords section of that CV, without asking, one keyword
  at a time until the CV fits on one page:
     1. First the generic keywords, those that name general
        data-handling methods, as opposed to modelling or analysis methods
        (today: Data Wrangling, ETL, Data Mining), from the end of the
        list. A generic keyword the posting asks for is not generic for
        that CV.
     2. Then the rest, from the end of the list (the least relevant, by
        rule 3.8).
  If it still does not fit, it removes the section. It says which
  keywords were dropped, or that the section was removed. The styled
  and ATS copies come from the same payload, so a cut needed by either
  one applies to both: they always carry the same keywords. cv.md
  keeps the full list.
- If the CV still runs past one page without Keywords, the agent proposes
  further cuts for that CV only, in this order, and asks before applying
  them:
     a. Third bullet of any job.
     b. Skills categories least relevant to the target roles, never
        Languages.
     c. Projects section.
     d. The second line of Awards & Honors.
     e. The UCM Student Tutor entry.
  Shortening or rewording a line to save space is also an ask-first cut.
  The agent may propose it at any point of this list, never before the
  Keywords cut.
- Facts flow one way: archive -> cv.md -> CVs. A wrong fact or wording
  found in a generated CV is corrected in the archive (if it is a fact)
  and in cv.md, never only in the payload, HTML or PDF. The one exception
  is the wording of a tailored summary (rule 3.5), which lives only in
  that CV's payload and is corrected there; a wrong fact in it is still
  corrected in the archive and cv.md.
